    findZero find x such that poly(x) = 0.
    findZero returns only one zero point, even if there are many.
    Moreover, findZero only takes list xs having even number of coefficients
    and largest non zero coefficient as it guarantees
    a solution.
    >>> findZero(Arrays.asList(1, 2)) // f(x) = 1 + 2x
    -0.5
    >>> findZero(Arrays.asList(-6, 11, -6, 1)) // (x - 1) * (x - 2) * (x - 3) = -6 + 11x - 6x^2 + x^3
    1.0
     */
    public double findZero(List<Double> xs) {
        // Use bisection method with bracketing
        // First, try to find an interval [a, b] where f(a) * f(b) < 0

        double left = -1000.0;
        double right = 1000.0;

        // Try to find a bracket by expanding outward if needed
        double fLeft = poly(xs, left);
        double fRight = poly(xs, right);

        // If we already have a sign change, proceed with bisection
        if (fLeft * fRight > 0) {
            // Try larger bounds
            for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 20; attempt++) {
                left *= 2;
                right *= 2;
                fLeft = poly(xs, left);
                fRight = poly(xs, right);
                if (fLeft * fRight <= 0) {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }

        // Bisection method
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) { // 100 iterations should be enough for double precision
            double mid = (left + right) / 2.0;
            double fMid = poly(xs, mid);

            if (Math.abs(fMid) < 1e-12) {
                return mid;
            }

            if (fLeft * fMid <= 0) {
                right = mid;
                fRight = fMid;
            } else {
                left = mid;
                fLeft = fMid;
            }
        }

        // Return midpoint of final interval
        return (left + right) / 2.0;
    }
}

